    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d352">

                <pb facs="0841" n="816" sameAs="frus:frus1958-60v08/pg816"/>

                <head>352. Memorandum of Conversation Between Secretary of State Herter and Foreign Secretary Lloyd<anchor n="0" xml:id="d352-f1"/></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName>Geneva</placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" type="conversation-or-meeting-date"
                            when="1959-06-02T12:00:00+01:00">June 2, 1959,
                    noon</date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p>I spoke to Selwyn Lloyd about the fact that
                    I was cabling the President<anchor n="1" xml:id="d352-f2"/> suggesting he make a
                    pretty strong statement at his press conference to the effect that nothing that
                    he had seen coming out of Geneva would in any way justify a Summit Conference.
                    To my great surprise, he thought this was an excellent idea and that it might
                    have great influence in changing the atmosphere here so as to give us more hope
                    of reaching some agreement that would justify a Summit Conference.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#bold"
                    >C.A.H.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d352-f1" n="0" type="source" xml:id="d352-f1-content">
                <p>Source: Department of State, Conference Files: Lot 64 D 560, CF 1364. Secret. Drafted by Herter. Two other memoranda of conversation, in
                    addition to Documents <ref target="#d353">353</ref> and <ref target="#d354"
                        >354</ref>, covering the London <hi rendition="#italic">Times</hi> article
                    and Hungarian credentials at the International Labor Organization are in
                    Department of State, Conference Files: Lot 64 D 560, CF 1364.</p>
            </note>
